The program counter of mrb_vm_exec() cached in a local, applied after wasm/vm-split.patch to the pinned mruby 4.0.0 source and only to the wasm build this repository converts to Go.
mruby 4.0 keeps the program counter of the running frame in ci->pc, so the dispatch loop reads it from memory to fetch the opcode, reads it again to decode the operands and writes it back advanced, once per instruction.
A native build holds that in a register; the converted Go build cannot, and a symbolized profile of this build attributes 58.5% of fib's samples to the linear-memory helpers (bounds check 22%, i32_store 21%, i32_load 11%, i32_load8_u 4%), with the ci->pc round-trip as the pattern that recurs at every opcode.
mruby before 3.x kept the program counter in a local with explicit sync points, so the shape is known-viable; this patch reintroduces it for this build only.

mrb_vm_exec() gains a local `vmpc` that holds the program counter of the running frame, and three macros: PC_SYNC() writes it to ci->pc, PC_RELOAD() adopts a ci->pc set elsewhere, PC_SET() stores a new value in both.
DECODE_OPERANDS() and CALL_CODE_HOOKS() work on `vmpc` instead of ci->pc, so the fetch-decode step of every instruction is register traffic.

Where ci->pc is made current is decided per dispatch macro rather than per opcode body:

- CASE() ends with PC_SYNC(), so every opcode body it labels sees exactly the ci->pc it saw before this patch. That is the default, and it leaves the opcode bodies unread and unedited: what the patch removes for them is the per-instruction load, not the store.
- CASE_F() omits the sync. The opcode bodies it labels are the ones audited to make no call that can raise, allocate, transfer control between callinfo frames or read ci->pc, so nothing between the operand decode and the next instruction can observe the stale value. They are OP_NOP, OP_MOVE, the OP_LOADI family, OP_LOADI16, OP_LOADSYM, OP_LOADNIL, OP_LOADSELF, OP_LOADTRUE, OP_LOADFALSE, the four jumps, OP_ADD/OP_SUB/OP_MUL, OP_ADDI/OP_SUBI and the five comparisons.
- JUMP is no longer a synonym for NEXT: it reloads `vmpc` from ci->pc first, and it is what every exit that changed ci->pc or switched callinfo frames uses (the send tail through VS_DISPATCH(), OP_CALL, OP_BLKCALL, OP_ENTER, OP_EXEC, the OP_RETURN family, OP_JMPUW, and the L_RAISE landing that OP_RAISEIF ends with, whose NEXT this patch turns into a JUMP).

The three windows where a CASE_F() body can still be observed are closed explicitly.
The code fetch hook fires with the same pc argument at the same points, and PC_SYNC() moved inside the `if ((mrb)->code_fetch_hook)` it already guards: the hook can raise, the raise path reads ci->pc of the interrupted frame, and a build with no hook armed pays nothing.
The arithmetic and comparison macros PC_SYNC() before every exit that leaves the integer or float fast path: the overflow branch, the boxed-float branch, the string branch and the operator-send fallback.
SET_INT_VALUE() heap-allocates outside the fixnum range under word boxing, which is the profile this build ships, so the integer result of OP_MATH/OP_MATHI goes through PC_SET_INT_VALUE(), which takes the immediate path when FIXABLE() holds and syncs before the allocating one. Under MRB_NO_BOXING the condition folds away.

OP_JMP, OP_JMPIF, OP_JMPNOT and OP_JMPNIL move the program counter in `vmpc` and fall through to NEXT, which is why they can be CASE_F(); OP_JMPUW keeps ci->pc because it reads it back for catch_handler_find() and can throw a tagged break.
OP_EXT1/OP_EXT2/OP_EXT3 decode into another opcode's body label, which skips that body's CASE() sync, so they use PC_SET().
`vmpc` needs no volatile: every path that re-enters the dispatch loop after a longjmp (L_RAISE, the RBREAK checkpoints) reaches it through JUMP.

